course: biology
lesson: 113
title: Breeding programmes for rare animals: studbooks and frozen zoos
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: If only a few snow leopards are left in zoos, they could soon all be cousins, and when close relatives have babies together, hidden illnesses in the family show up much more often. So zoos plan carefully which animals should become parents.
explain: Every snow leopard in zoos is written into one giant family tree called a studbook, and a computer program looks for the pair that is least related, even if the male lives in one country and the female in another.
explain: Some zoos keep a «frozen zoo»: tanks of liquid nitrogen at minus 196 degrees full of cells, eggs and sperm, like a library of genes. In 2020 a Przewalski's horse foal called Kurt was cloned from cells frozen 40 years earlier.
explain: Good zoos breed to keep rare animals healthy and varied, not just to make cute babies for the news, and that is why some animals in zoos are not bred at all.
fact: Zoos swap animals between countries through planned breeding programmes, called EEPs in Europe and SSPs in North America.
fact: When close relatives have babies together, inherited illnesses become more common, so swapping animals keeps the family tree healthy.
fact: A studbook records every animal of one kind in zoos: its parents, birth, moves and offspring; some go back over a hundred years.
fact: Computer programs find pairs that are least related, so the population keeps many different genes and can cope with disease and change; sometimes the chosen pair do not get along and keepers must try again.
fact: Frozen zoos keep cells, eggs and sperm of rare animals at minus 196 degrees in liquid nitrogen; San Diego's Frozen Zoo holds cells from over a thousand kinds of animals.
fact: In 2020 a Przewalski's horse foal called Kurt was cloned from cells frozen in 1980, bringing back genes lost from the living herd; a clone has the same genes as the animal whose cells were used, and cloning needs well-kept cells, so a stone fossil cannot be cloned.
fact: Black-footed ferrets were thought extinct until a small group was found in Wyoming in 1981; they were bred and released, and a clone called Elizabeth Ann, made from cells frozen in the 1980s, was born in 2020.
fact: Zoos sometimes stop breeding an animal when there is no space or no need, and some use birth control to manage numbers.
fact: Births of rare animals like gorillas or rhinos are celebrated, because a small population needs every healthy baby.
fact: If a mother cannot care for her baby, keepers may hand-rear it, but they try to keep it with its own kind, because a chick raised only by people may not act like its own kind or fear danger in the wild; condor chicks were fed with puppets shaped like condor heads.
fact: Eggs in incubators are safe from predators, and in some reptiles, such as tortoises, the warmth of the egg decides whether the baby is male or female.
fact: Some rare birds lay a second egg if the first is taken, so a zoo can hatch two chicks instead of one.
fact: Many zoo animals, such as meerkats, are not endangered, and critics say breeding them helps only the zoo; some zoos focus on rare small animals like frogs, snails and insects.
exercise: Why do zoos send animals to other countries to breed? | To avoid close relatives pairing and keep the family tree healthy | Because animals get bored of living in their own country for too long | To make the animals learn new languages | Because every zoo must swap once a year
exercise: What are Europe's zoo breeding programmes called? | EEPs | SSPs | AZAs | NHSs
exercise: What is a studbook? | A record of every animal of one kind in zoos, with parents and babies | A book of funny stories about zoo animals | A diary kept by the head keeper | A list of the zoo's opening times
exercise: How do scientists choose which two animals should breed? | A computer program finds the pair least related | The two biggest and strongest animals are always chosen | Visitors vote for the cutest pair | The oldest male picks his partner
exercise: What happens if the chosen pair do not get along? | The keepers try another pair | They are forced to live together anyway | Breeding of that kind stops forever | The computer is thrown away for good
exercise: How cold is a frozen zoo kept? | Minus 196 degrees, in liquid nitrogen | Minus 4 degrees, like a home freezer | Zero degrees, like the ice on a pond | Minus 1,000 degrees, colder than space
exercise: What is kept in a frozen zoo? | Cells, eggs and sperm of rare animals | Whole frozen animals for visitors to see | Ice lollies kept for the hungry keepers | Frozen fish for the polar bears in summer
exercise: Who was Kurt? | A Przewalski's horse foal cloned in 2020 from cells frozen in 1980 | The first panda born in London Zoo | A keeper who invented the studbook | A black-footed ferret found alive in Wyoming in 1981 after being lost
exercise: Why was Kurt useful to the living herd? | His old cells held genes the living herd had lost | He was much faster and stronger than all the other horses | He could live for two hundred years | He was the only male horse left
exercise: What happened to the black-footed ferret in 1981? | A small group was found in Wyoming after it was thought extinct | It was declared extinct for ever | It was first discovered and named by scientists in that very year | It escaped from San Diego Zoo
exercise: Why do some zoos use birth control for animals? | To manage numbers when there is no space or no need | To make the animals grow bigger and stronger than wild ones | Because babies are too noisy | To stop visitors seeing babies
exercise: Why is a gorilla birth at a zoo big news? | A small population of a rare animal needs every healthy baby | Gorillas are born only once a century | The baby is always named after the mayor | Gorilla babies are born able to talk
exercise: How were baby condors fed so they would not get used to people? | With puppets shaped like condor heads | With a bottle held by a famous actor | By a robot dressed as a keeper | They were not fed until they could fly
exercise: What can the warmth of an incubator decide in tortoise eggs? | Whether the babies are male or female | What colour the shells will be when they hatch | How many legs the babies have | Which language they will understand
exercise: How can taking one egg from a rare bird give two chicks? | Some birds lay a second egg when the first is taken | The egg splits neatly into two in the warm incubator after a week | Keepers glue two halves together | The bird lays an egg every hour
exercise: What do critics say about breeding animals that are not endangered? | It helps the zoo more than the wild | It is the most important job in conservation | It saves more species than anything else | Nobody has ever criticised it at all
test: «my cousin says it's fine for brother and sister tigers to have cubs, they're animals innit» What is right? | Close relatives pairing makes inherited illnesses more common | It makes the cubs stronger and healthier than cubs of strangers | Animals cannot inherit any illnesses | Tigers can only breed with relatives
test: What would happen to a small group of animals if it bred only among itself for many generations? | It would lose genes and become less able to cope with disease | It would gain lots of new genes every year from its own members | It would become a bigger species | Nothing would ever change
test: «why'd a tiger come all the way from another country, we got loads of tigers here» What is the best answer? | A tiger from far away is less related to ours, keeping the family tree healthy | Foreign tigers are a different, bigger and much better kind of tiger than our own ones | Our tigers are all too old to walk | It came for a holiday
test: In the film Jurassic Park scientists bring back dinosaurs from old blood. What is true about real cloning in zoos? | It uses well-kept frozen cells of animals alive today or recently | Any extinct animal can be cloned from a fossil | Clones grow into adults in a day | Cloning makes the animal smarter and stronger than its parent was
test: «so a clone's like a twin born later?» Is that a fair way to say it? | Yes, a clone has the same genes as the animal whose cells were used | No, a clone has completely new genes | No, a clone is a robot copy | No, a clone is always a different species from the animal it came from
test: A hand-reared chick follows people everywhere. What problem could it have in the wild? | It may not behave like its own kind or fear danger | It will fly too fast to land | It will never be hungry | It will be too clean to survive
test: Two snow leopards were picked by the computer, but they snarl at each other every day. What should the keepers do? | Try another pair from the studbook | Keep them together until they like each other | Stop breeding snow leopards forever | Paint them to look like other leopards
test: «a zoo breeds meerkats for babies on the news, that's conservation right» What is a fair answer? | Not always; good breeding helps rare animals, but meerkats are not endangered | Yes, every baby born at a zoo helps save its species | Yes, the news is how species are saved | No, zoos never breed any animal at all
test: «4 baby giant tortoises hatched, how many legs in total? mate says 8» | 16, four legs each | 8, two legs each, like birds | 4, just one leg each | 12, three legs each
test: Why would a family tree help save a species? | It shows who is related so keepers avoid pairing close relatives | It helps keepers choose good family names for all the new babies | It shows which animal is the oldest only | It is needed to sell the animals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a studbook? | It records the parentage, birth, movements and offspring of every animal of a given species in zoos | It records the opinions of visitors concerning each animal | It sets out the feeding regime of a single enclosure | It lists animals available for private purchase
exam: Identify the principal reason for which zoological institutions avoid the pairing of closely related animals. | Such pairings increase the incidence of inherited disease and reduce genetic variety | Such pairings are prohibited solely on grounds of the cost of transporting the animals | Related animals are incapable of producing offspring | Such pairings produce offspring of excessive size
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Przewalski's horse designated Kurt. | It was cloned in 2020 from cells cryopreserved in 1980 | It was the last wild horse captured in Mongolia in 1969 | It was born naturally at London Zoo in 1828 | It was cloned from a fossil recovered in Siberia
exam: State the temperature at which genetic material is customarily preserved in a frozen zoo. | Minus one hundred and ninety-six degrees Celsius | Minus four degrees Celsius | Zero degrees Celsius | Minus nineteen degrees Celsius
